The original accepted software bytes were not modified. No Docker container was
started and no image was built.

All three frozen cases completed sequentially:

| Case | Validated worker result | Completed rank-proof counts |
| --- | --- | ---: |
| Wrong control pin | `control_binding_rejected`, before ML | 0 |
| Wrong adapter pin | `adapter_binding_rejected`, before ML | 0 |
| Positive WorkerRun | `passed` / `ok` | 6 each |

The four positive totals are controller agreements, pre-ML rank checks, worker
admissions and worker constructors. The pinned adapter validated the full private
observer and its frozen deterministic resume requirements before emitting these
small receipts. The observer was not retained, so the local audit validates the
receipts and source-bound validation path, not a second execution of WorkerRun.
`parent_ml_import_absent=true` concerns the privileged parent; the positive CPU
synthetic workload did execute its approved ML fixture, not a pretrained model or
real SFT. Acquisition's `worker_dispatched=false` is an acquisition-stage claim,
not a claim that the later native fixture did not execute.

Each case attests actual workload cgroup containment, guardian-parent binding,
one owned-cgroup kill, process reaping and empty/removed cgroup state. The final
native receipt also verifies owned private cleanup and final inventories proving
the owned image/container absent. A separate receipt verifies retirement of the
single exact acquired input file and its parent directory.

The nested extraction summary was taken **before** final cleanup: its
